[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (8) The amount which the litigation friend may recover under paragraph (1) in respect of costs must not (in proceedings at first instance) exceed 25% of the amount of the sum agreed or awarded in respect of—

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (a) general damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity; and

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (b) damages for past financial loss

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (9) Except in a case to which Section IV, Section VI, Section VII or Section VIII of Part 45 applies, and  where a claim under rule 45.9 or 45.10 has not been made, no application may be made under this rule for a payment out of the money recovered by the child or protected party until the costs payable to the child or protected party have been assessed or agreed. [PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (10) A litigation friend must support a claim for payment from a fund of costs or expenses by filing a witness statement setting out, so far as applicable—

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (a) the nature and amount of the costs or expenses and the reason they were incurred;

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (b) a copy of any conditional fee or damages based agreement;

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (c) a copy of any risk assessment by reference to which any success fee was determined;

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (d) the reasons why the particular funding model was selected;

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (e) the advice given to the litigation friend on funding arrangements;

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (f) a copy bill or informal breakdown of the solicitor and own client base costs incurred;

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (g) details of any costs agreed, recovered or fixed costs recoverable by the child; and

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (h) an explanation of the amount agreed or awarded for—

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (i) general damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity; and

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.12] (ii) damages for past financial loss, net of any sums recoverable by the Compensation Recovery Unit or the Department for Work and Pensions. ## Appointment of a guardian of a child’s estate

## 21.13

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.13] (1) The court may appoint the Official Solicitor to be a guardian of a child’s estate where –

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.13] (a) money is paid into court on behalf of the child in accordance with directions given under rule 21.11 (control of money received by a child or protected party);

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.13] (b) the Criminal Injuries Compensation Authority notifies the court that it has made or intends to make an award to the child;

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.13] (c) a court or tribunal outside England and Wales notifies the court that it has ordered or intends to order that money be paid to the child;

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.13] (d) the child is absolutely entitled to the proceeds of a pension fund; or

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.13] (e) in any other case, such an appointment seems desirable to the court. [PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.13] (2) The court may not appoint the Official Solicitor under this rule unless –

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.13] (a) the persons with parental responsibility (within the meaning of section 3 of the Children Act 1989 2 ) agree; or

[PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.13] (b) the court considers that their agreement can be dispensed with. [PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.13] (3) The Official Solicitor’s appointment may continue only until the child reaches 18. [PART 21 – CHILDREN AND PROTECTED PARTIES > 21.13] (Paragraph 8 of Practice Direction 40A deals with approval of the accounts of a guardian of assets of a child.)

